    <section id="why-switch">
      <h2>Why Groups Look For A Discord Music Bot Alternative</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bot setup friction</strong> - commands, permissions, and channel routing get in the way of session start.</li>
        <li><strong>Split focus</strong> - running voice, text, and music all through the same interface can feel messy during live play.</li>
        <li><strong>Poor session flow</strong> - scene changes take too many clicks when the music tool is not built around tabletop pacing.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="better-fit">
      <h2>When Far Reach Radio Is The Better Fit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You want one dedicated music surface</strong> - open one host panel and keep soundtrack control separate from table chat.</li>
        <li><strong>You want synced playback</strong> - listeners hear the same stream timeline instead of individually managing tracks.</li>
        <li><strong>You want no player accounts</strong> - send one link and let players join quickly.</li>
        <li><strong>You want scene-ready playlists</strong> - switch between combat, town, horror, travel, and victory music without rebuilding setup.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="workflow">
      <h2>How The Workflow Changes</h2>
      <figure class="toolshot-card toolshot-card--focus">
