          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Dedicated host controls for session music, separate from your chat or voice stack.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Far Reach Radio:</strong> create a channel, choose a playlist, copy the listener link, and start the session.</li>
        <li><strong>Far Reach Radio:</strong> keep Discord for voice and text if you want, while music runs through its own cleaner surface.</li>
        <li><strong>Result:</strong> less audio-management overhead when the GM needs to move fast during scenes.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="discord-still-works">
      <h2>When Discord Music Bots May Still Be Enough</h2>
      <p>This is not a claim that every group needs a separate music tool.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Casual use</strong> - if you only need occasional background tracks and your current bot works, switching may not matter.</li>
        <li><strong>All-in-one preference</strong> - some groups would rather keep every tool inside Discord even if the workflow is rougher.</li>
        <li><strong>No dedicated soundtrack habit</strong> - if music is optional at your table, a simpler all-purpose tool may be enough.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

